# Line endings are decided here, by the repository, and not by whatever
# core.autocrlf each machine happens to have set.
#
# Until now this repository relied on a local `core.autocrlf=true`. That works
# for whoever set it and for nobody else: a fresh clone on a machine without it
# commits CRLF into the index, and the next diff shows every line of the file as
# changed. The index is currently uniform LF, and this file is what keeps it that
# way without depending on a setting no clone inherits.
#
# It also stops a file being misclassified. `docs/BUILD_TEST_RELEASE.md` had
# picked up three doubled CRs (`\r\r\n`, what a tool leaves when it rewrites an
# already-CRLF file as though it were LF). Git reads a lone CR as a sign of
# binary content, so it stopped diffing that document altogether — a build and
# release procedure whose changes had become invisible in review.

# The default: normalise to LF in the repository, check out in the platform's
# own convention. Everything not named below is covered by this.
* text=auto

# LF in the working tree, on every platform. A shell script with CRLF fails as
# `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`, and both CI workflows run steps with
# `shell: bash`, including on windows-latest.
*.sh text eol=lf

# CRLF in the working tree. Visual Studio and PowerShell tooling rewrite these
# files with CRLF whenever they touch them; without the rule, a developer whose
# core.autocrlf is off gets a whole-file diff the first time they open one.
*.sln  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never converted, never diffed as text, never merged line by line.
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.apk  binary
*.keystore binary

# The project's own binary formats. Gitignored today, declared here anyway: an
# ignore rule is one `git add -f` away from being bypassed, and a conversion
# applied to a capture file or a key would corrupt it silently.
*.noscap binary
*.NOS    binary
*.gguf   binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.dpapi  binary

# The vendored WinDivert driver and its loader. Declared rather than left to
# git's own detection: core.autocrlf is true on the machine that committed them,
# detection is a heuristic, and a signed kernel driver that acquires a single
# translated line ending stops loading. The failure would surface as
# driver_signature_rejected, which reads like a problem with the machine.
*.dll    binary
*.sys    binary

# Generated, and not worth reading in a review or counting as authored code.
proto/**/*_pb2.py     linguist-generated=true
proto/**/*_pb2.pyi    linguist-generated=true
